================================================================================================
DOES A HIDDEN-SUBGROUP HAMILTONIAN HAVE A GRADIENT SIGNATURE?
================================================================================================
  N = 6. Diagonal H = sum_x f(x)|x><x|; fhat(S) ARE H's Pauli-Z coefficients.

  hidden shift s = 101101

    Simon-structured :  32 / 64 Pauli strings nonzero   ( 50.0%)
                       orthogonal to s :  32    NOT orthogonal :   0
    generic diagonal :  64 / 64 Pauli strings nonzero   (100.0%)
                       orthogonal to s :  32    NOT orthogonal :  32

  A Simon-structured H should keep exactly the strings T with T.s = 0,
  which is half of them, and kill every other. That is the signature.

================================================================================================
DOES THE GRADIENT INHERIT IT?
================================================================================================
                        ||grad||   max |g_i|   min |g_i|  zero comps
  ------------------------------------------------------------------
    Simon-structured     0.61935     0.24318    0.00e+00           6
    generic diagonal     0.66960     0.31134    0.00e+00           6

  READING IT. If the Pauli support collapses to the annihilator subgroup
  but the GRADIENT looks statistically ordinary, then the structure is
  real and present in H yet invisible in the quantity an optimiser reads.
  That is the sharpest form of the obstruction: the periodicity lives in
  the Pauli list, which you must already know to specify the problem, and
  the ansatz scrambles it out of the landscape. Shor escapes only because
  its function arrives as an ORACLE whose period is not written in the
  circuit that evaluates it.
